    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "At Favignana Island (southern Italy), several quarries provide an excellent 3D view of Lower- Pleistocene grainstones crosscut by a strike-slip fault system. This fault system is made up of two conjugate sets of strike-slip structural features such as Compactive Shear Bands, Zones of Compactive Shear Bands and larger faults with discrete slip surfaces. This contribution integrates structural analysis and numerical modeling to build up a 3D Discrete Fracture Network model, which is used for fluid flow simulations of a carbonate reservoir analogue.\r\n\r\nThis new workflow appears to be promising for reservoir-scale assessment of sub-seismic structures and their impact on the bulk permeability of porous carbonate reservoirs.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Cilona, Antonino, et. al. (2013). Nature and Distribution of Strike-Slip Faults Crosscutting Carbonate Grainstones (Favignana, Southern Italy) and Their Impact on Fluid Flow. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at: http://purl.stanford.edu/rs041zb7582",
